      "The first structural vulnerability lies in 'emotional entanglement and the conflation of family entitlement with executive merit'. Within closely held family dynasties, parents frequently hesitate to evaluate their children's managerial competencies objectively, while sibling rivalries or cousin-consortium factions turn executive appointments into symbolic referendums on parental affection and branch status. Appointing a family heir to the chief executive office without rigorous external benchmarking risks alienating high-performing non-family executives, who conclude that senior leadership remains a closed hereditary preserve.",
      "Simultaneously, the absence of a codified 'ownership exit and liquidity architecture' threatens institutional stability whenever non-operating family shareholders diverge in their financial expectations. While active family executives typically favour retaining earnings to fund multi-year technological modernisation, passive third-generation shareholders—often scattered across continents with distinct lifestyle and tax needs—frequently demand higher dividend distributions or seek to monetise their equity stakes. Without a structured share-redemption mechanism or family liquidity pool, dissatisfied relatives may sell voting shares to opportunistic external funds, triggering hostile governance deadlocks.",
      "Furthermore, incumbent patriarchs and matriarchs often experience acute 'psychological identity fusion' with the enterprise they built, leading to chronic succession procrastination. Even after formally naming a successor, retiring founders frequently retain overlapping chairmanships, informal veto powers over capital expenditure, and back-channel relationships with long-standing divisional heads—undermining the incoming leader's legitimacy and creating paralyzing dual-command structures.",
