"texte": [
"Macroeconomic & Industrial Policy Essay — Over the past four decades, advanced economies have experienced a profound structural shift commonly termed 'corporate financialisation': the growing dominance of financial motives, financial markets, financial actors, and financial metrics over real industrial production within non-financial corporations. Whereas mid-twentieth-century industrial enterprises retained the overwhelming majority of operating cash flows to reinvest in physical plants, proprietary research, and workforce skills, major listed industrial groups across North America and Europe now routinely distribute between 80 % and 110 % of net income to shareholders via dividends and share buybacks—frequently funding capital returns through corporate borrowing while net fixed-capital formation stagnates.",
"The first macroeconomic consequence of corporate financialisation lies in 'the decoupling of financial returns from real productive investment'. Empirical studies of non-financial corporations demonstrate that as executive remuneration became tightly indexed to short-term stock-price performance and return on equity ('ROE'), capital allocation tilted systematically away from irreversible, long-gestation industrial assets—such as semiconductor fabs, clean-steel plants, and grid infrastructure—toward liquid financial assets, M&A roll-ups, and share repurchases that boost financial ratios without expanding aggregate productive capacity.",
"Simultaneously, the proliferation of 'private-equity leveraged buyouts and asset-light restructuring models' has reshaped corporate balance sheets across retail, healthcare, manufacturing, and infrastructure. By splitting integrated operating businesses into separate property-holding entities ('OpCo/PropCo' structures), loading operating subsidiaries with high-yield debt, and extracting management monitoring fees, financialised ownership models often convert resilient cash-generating enterprises into fragile entities burdened by heavy fixed lease and interest obligations—leaving scant margin for research, wage growth, or cyclical downturns.",
"Furthermore, financialisation has altered 'corporate treasury behaviour and supply-chain power dynamics'. Many cash-rich multinational corporations now operate de facto internal hedge funds—managing vast portfolios of sovereign debt, money-market instruments, and currency carry trades—while simultaneously stretching supplier payment terms from thirty to one hundred and twenty days ('reverse factoring' and supply-chain finance) to extract free working-capital liquidity from smaller industrial subcontractors.",
"Macroeconomic evidence increasingly confirms that excessive financialisation carries severe long-term costs for national competitiveness, supply-chain resilience, and productivity growth. Economies that prioritise financial engineering over industrial reinvestment experience slower total-factor productivity growth, heightened import dependence for critical manufactured goods, and sharper vulnerability to geopolitical supply shocks.",
"The subtle irony of corporate financialisation is that financial markets ultimately depend on the real productive economy for the cash flows that underpin asset valuations; yet by rewarding executives for extracting cash rather than reinvesting in productive capabilities, financialised governance steadily erodes the industrial base upon which financial returns depend.",
"Reversing the excesses of financialisation and re-anchoring capital markets in the service of the productive economy requires four coordinated institutional and corporate governance reforms: first, revising tax and regulatory incentives to favour long-term productive capital expenditure and R&D over debt-financed share repurchases; second, aligning executive incentive plans with multi-year return on invested capital ('ROIC'), productivity gains, and organic innovation metrics rather than leveraged ROE; third, enforcing transparent disclosure of supply-chain payment terms to curb working-capital extraction from small suppliers; and fourth, fostering patient institutional capital vehicles dedicated to industrial modernisation.",
"Ultimately, finance is an indispensable servant of economic progress when it channels savings into productive enterprise, but a destructive master when financial engineering becomes an end in itself."
