"titre2": "Exercise 19",
"texte": [
"Capital Markets & Institutional Finance Briefing — Prepared for the Corporate Treasury & Strategy Council of Continental Mid-Market Industrial Alliance — Over the past decade, the global corporate financing landscape has undergone a profound structural disintermediation: while commercial banks have steadily retreated from leveraged middle-market lending, project finance, and complex corporate credit under tighter post-crisis capital adequacy rules ('Basel III' and 'Basel IV'), 'alternative investment funds'—encompassing private credit direct lenders, private equity buyout funds, distressed-debt special-situations funds, and infrastructure credit vehicles—have surged to manage over $15 trillion globally, fundamentally transforming how mid-sized and large enterprises raise capital.",
"The primary catalyst behind the ascent of 'private credit and direct-lending funds' lies in speed, execution certainty, and bespoke structural flexibility. Whereas arranging a syndicated bank loan across six commercial banks requires months of credit-committee approvals, public syndication risk, and rigid amortisation schedules, a large direct-lending fund can underwrite a €350 million unitranche facility in three weeks, tailor covenant packages to complex acquisition timelines, and hold the loan to maturity without market-flex pricing surprises.",
"However, this transformation of financing channels carries substantial 'cost, covenant, and governance trade-offs' that corporate borrowers frequently underestimate during benign credit markets. Private credit facilities typically carry floating interest rates priced 250 to 450 basis points above comparable syndicated bank loans, include strict call-protection prepayment penalties during the first three years, and grant direct lenders sweeping affirmative covenants, board observer seats, and aggressive enforcement remedies—including swift equity-pledge seizure—if borrower performance falters.",
"Simultaneously, the rapid expansion of 'non-bank financial intermediation' ('shadow banking') has introduced novel systemic and refinancing vulnerabilities into corporate credit markets. Unlike commercial banks backed by stable retail deposit bases and central-bank lender-of-last-resort liquidity facilities, alternative credit funds rely on closed-end institutional commitments or semi-liquid redemption vehicles whose fundraising slows sharply during macroeconomic downturns—raising the risk that borrowers relying exclusively on private credit may face sudden capital rationing when maturities fall due.",
"Furthermore, in the equity sphere, the dominance of 'mega-buyout and growth-equity funds' has altered corporate lifecycle dynamics and public-market depth. By keeping high-growth technology and industrial champions private for twelve to fifteen years through successive secondary buyouts ('GP-led continuation vehicles'), alternative funds insulate management from quarterly stock-market scrutiny, yet simultaneously concentrate ownership control, impose high management and carried-interest fee layers, and limit public-market investor participation.",
